        "id": 1137063
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-09-18T00:17:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "That suggestion still doesn\u0027t make sense to me.  There already is a way to specify other kinds of interrupts: \"interrupt-ranges\" and \"interrupt-templates\".  And from what I understood from what Marc said, I don\u0027t think he meant the detail of \"IPIs are specified by using 1 argument to irq_create_of_mapping()\" to be something that\u0027s exposed in device tree files, as that means that it can never be changed later.  Instead the device tree should just specify the IPI numbers, and the way of doing the mapping should be a driver implementation detail.\n\nmzyngier@, can you confirm whether I\u0027m understanding correctly?",
      "parentUuid": "c7aa4c53_354e78aa",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 34,
        "startChar": 2,
        "endLine": 34,
        "endChar": 5
      },
      "revId": "9003bbc3c2b12ae48f2c4638c80e1fb57bd8fb42",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "125d1702_f41017fa",
        "filename": "Documentation/devicetree/bindings/trusty/trusty-irq.txt",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 34,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000205
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-09-18T00:55:24Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "That is how all other gic interrupts work though. If you add a parameter to the gic other interrupts you have to update device trees. If the IPIs exposed by the gic driver change to have more parameters something has to be updated. We can either update the device tree entry, which is where we have the other gic specific information, or the trusty-irq driver, which currently does not have any gic specific code. If IPIs are the only interrupts that can work without any additional parameters, then your current name makes sense, but I did not think that was the case.",
      "parentUuid": "c024be4b_b4107fc6",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 34,
        "startChar": 2,
        "endLine": 34,
        "endChar": 5
      },
      "revId": "9003bbc3c2b12ae48f2c4638c80e1fb57bd8fb42",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "cceccba4_5dff8b61",
        "filename": "Documentation/devicetree/bindings/trusty/trusty-irq.txt",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 34,
      "author": {
        "id": 1137063
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-09-18T18:40:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The GIC interrupt specifiers can\u0027t be changed because the upstream kernel considers device trees to be a stable interface.  Device tree files may be outside the kernel source tree, and people may use old device tree files with new kernels.  So whatever the current way of specifying interrupts for the GIC is, that has to be permanently supported in all future kernel versions.\n\nWe may still be willing to accept breaking changes in the Trusty bindings for now.  However, we should still avoid defining an interface that directly exposes internal kernel implementation details.  If I understand correctly, Marc did *not* intend for the way to call irq_create_of_mapping() to map a SGI to be a stable interface which is guaranteed to stay the same in all future kernel versions.  Therefore it should be handled in the driver, not the device tree.  The device tree should just list the IPI numbers directly.\n\nAlso note that if there is an interrupt controller whose interrupt specifiers take only one cell, the existing \"interrupt-templates\" will work fine for it.  There\u0027s no need to re-use the IPI property for that case.",
      "parentUuid": "125d1702_f41017fa",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 34,
        "startChar": 2,
        "endLine": 34,
        "endChar": 5
      },
      "revId": "9003bbc3c2b12ae48f2c4638c80e1fb57bd8fb42",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
